Using type assertion `e as Error` doesn't guarantee that `e` is actually an Error instance. In JavaScript, catch blocks can catch any value (string, number, object, etc.).

Consider checking the error type:
```typescript
catch (e) {
  const error = e instanceof Error ? e : new Error(String(e));
  this.logger.error('Find operation failed', error, { object });
  throw error;
}
```

This pattern appears throughout this file in multiple catch blocks (lines 367, 402, 453, 491, 529).

