```

The caught error is passed directly to the logger without ensuring it's an Error object. In JavaScript, catch blocks can catch any value (string, number, object, etc.), not just Error instances.

Consider checking the error type before passing it to the logger:
```typescript
catch (error) {
  const err = error instanceof Error ? error : new Error(String(error));
  this.logger?.error?.('MSW: Update failed', err, { object, id });
  // ... rest of the code
}
```

The caught error `e` (typed as `any`) is passed directly to the logger without ensuring it's an Error object. In JavaScript, catch blocks can catch any value, not just Error instances.

Consider ensuring the error is an Error instance:
```typescript
catch(e:any) { 
  const error = e instanceof Error ? e : new Error(String(e));
  ctx.logger.error('Data find failed', error, { object: req.params.object });
  // ... rest
}
```

